    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Has a Taste of the e-MTB World with the RN0 Motor-Assisted Bicycle\nGeneral Motors Canada Ending Chevrolet Silverado 1500 Production at Oshawa\n7 photos\nPhoto: Chevrolet\nOshawa Assembly will drop the Silverado 1500 by the end of December 2026. The Ontario facility will continue building heavy-duty pickups, though, starting with the 2027 model year Silverado HD. Later on, the Canadian plant will take on the next-generation Chevrolet Silverado HD and its GMC-branded sibling.\nGeneral Motors Canada spokesperson Ariane Souza Pereira clarified to Automotive News that production will continue on two shifts, including for the complex transitions to the redesigned Silverado HD and Sierra HD. The Detroit-based automaker will spend approximately CAD 500 million by 2030 on manufacturing upgrades and the next-generation HD truck program.\nTotal investments in the facility have reached CAD 1.5 billion since 2020. Part of the plant will also continue producing aftermarket components through stamping and subassembly work. Looking at the bigger picture, the loss of the Silverado 1500 does leave a sizable hole to fill.\nOshawa Assembly produced 70,551 vehicles during the first nine months of 2026, down from 95,041 over the same period last year. Said 26 percent nosedive can also be explained by GM's elimination of the third shift in January 2026, which affected 700 workers. Once next-gen production starts, the facility is expected to output in the ballpark of 100,000 Silverado HDs and Sierra HDs annually.\nThat would be just around half the plant's capacity, but remember that eventual output will depend on consumer demand across North America. Tariffs will play a role as well, and hopefully, the US-Canada tariff situation will be resolved after the 120th Congress convenes in January 2027 with Dems in control of the House and the Senate.\nPhoto: GM GM hasn't provided a debut date for the next-gen trucks, but Sam Fiorani of AutoForecast Solutions is betting that both models will start production at Oshawa in the earliest part of 2029. Considering that General Motors already replaced the L5P engine with the 8.3-liter Duramax, the next generation is likely going to steal customers from both the Ford Super Duty and Ram's HD line of workhorses.\nFrom 2027, the Silverado 1500 will use plants in the United States and Mexico. Those factories are Fort Wayne Assembly in Indiana, the Orion Township facility in Michigan, and Silao Assembly in Guanajuato. The latter had the largest output in 2025 among the three, operating at full capacity to assemble more than 300,000 examples of the Chevrolet Silverado 1500 and GMC Sierra 1500.\nFort Wayne Assembly isn't far behind in terms of annual capacity. Orion, meanwhile, was initially supposed to build electric trucks. The production schedule was pushed back time and time again, with General Motors eventually announcing that internal combustion trucks would be produced there from 2027. Based on reliable info, Orion Assembly will start churning out half-ton pickups in the second quarter of 2027.\nFor Oshawa Assembly , its future depends on how two heavy-duty trucks compete in a segment dominated by the Detroit Big Three. The Ford Motor Company leads the segment as of November 2026, boasting 40,000 pounds (18,144 kilograms) of max gooseneck towing capacity and 7,900 (3,583) of max payload.\nVia // Automotive News\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Silverado 1500 Chevrolet Silverado HD chevrolet silverado Chevrolet GM production\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2027 Toyota Sienna Released with Tons of Changes, Throws More Power to the Road",
